Autonomous robots increasingly operate in remote, infrastructure-poor, and disaster-affected environments where Low Earth Orbit (LEO) satellite networks are becoming the primary connectivity option. Existing robotic systems treat the link as an external condition observed and reacted to at run time, an abstraction inherited from terrestrial wireless, where a moving terminal connects to fixed infrastructure. But this abstraction obscures a structural property of the setting: connectivity arises from the joint motion of two bodies whose trajectories are each largely predictable in their own regimes (orbital mechanics on the satellite side, mission state and motion models on the robot side), at horizons that overlap with autonomy planning. In this position paper, we argue that satellite-assisted robotics should treat future LEO connectivity as a planned resource that robots co-plan with the network, rather than as a passive pipe or random disruption. We identify five mismatches between robotic autonomy assumptions and LEO behavior, propose a five-stage autonomy loop (Predict, Sense, Summarize, Schedule, Act) that operationalizes the position, and trace three design moves that follow: the planner state includes a forecast of future contact, communication units and task formulation are conditional on predicted contact, and multi-robot coordination operates over a forecastable, time-varying contact graph.